  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The parabola ${y}^{2}-3x+12y+36=0$ intersects the $x$-axis at the point$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-3x+12y+36 & =0 \\\\ (0)^{2}-3x+12(0)+36 & =0 \\\\ -3x+36 & =0 \\\\ -3x & =-36 \\\\ x & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at $(12,0).$",
